A||||9||Tonker||What's a normal oil pressure?||||1||
Z||000000||Tonker||09-04-2001||11:20 PM||||Ok, I really suck. I don't even know what unit of measurement my Oil Pressure guage uses( it goes from 0-550 ). Anyways, what should my guage be indicating when the car is running. I just changed the oil sending unit. It used to sit at about 275 when idling, then go up to about 500 or so when I hit the highway. Now when I start the car up and it idles - it's been sitting for a few days - at 550. Is that normal or have I screwed something up? Haven't yet taken it out for a test since I wanted to hear what you guys think first...||||reg||1
Z||000001||Joe Torma||09-04-2001||11:44 PM||||As far as I know you're supposed to have at least 10psi for every 1000 rpm.  What are the units you're using on that dial?<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/simplesig.gif"> <BR>Did you get your car in my book yet????<BR>http://www.animatedconcepts.com/fierobook.htm||||reg||1
Z||000002||Tonker||09-04-2001||11:53 PM||||I don't know what unit the guage uses. It's the one in my '86 GT's dash. My best guess is that since we're using metric up here that it's in Kpa or KiloPascals.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Joe Torma||09-05-2001||02:24 AM||||Sounds high, but since you haven't driven it ...I think it will drop to normal pressures when its warm.||||reg||1
Z||000004||JohnF||09-05-2001||07:43 AM||||I won't even try to do the math but...<P>"Normal" oil pressure is above 20 psi - period!<P>My '88 Duke runs +20 psi at idle and rises to a steady +60 psi at cruise (with the 5W - 30W factory recommended oil viscosity).<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||87GTZ34||09-05-2001||09:04 AM||||Tonker it should at least fluctuate when you rev it up.  If it doesn't then you may have the two wires on the sender backwards.  One contact is an on/off for the idiot light, and the other varies w/ pressure for the gauge.  If the needle justs sits there when you turn the ignition on and stays there when you start it that's a good sign that you need to switch them.  This of course is only true if your connector has crumbled out of existence like mine has and wires are separated.||||reg||1
Z||000006||BlackARROW||09-05-2001||11:12 AM||||Tanker, I don't know what unit they are using for our side.  It doesn't look like Pascals.<P>Well on my side, my Duke goes to 550 often as I nail it from time to time  (between 7 to 7 the next morning  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> but normally at idle, it stays at about 3 quarter of the gage.  I guess we should use the unit "quarters of the gage" in order to find out.<P><P>------------------<BR>BA-&gt;<BR>Gadgeteered Fiero 2M4 '85||||reg||6
Z||000007||Old Lar||09-05-2001||01:31 PM||||psi.........Kpa (Kilopascal)<BR>10..........69<BR>20..........138<BR>40..........276<BR>60..........414<BR>80..........552<P>At least that is what my conversion program gives me.||||reg||1
Z||000008||rogergarrison||09-05-2001||10:56 PM||||anything above the lower red line is ok at idle. Normal driving should be around 30-60 psi at all speeds. Every car will be different. On startup it will be high, near or at top of scale, but soon as engine warms up it will drop down. Long drives faster or in hotter weather than normal will make it drop slightly too. Lower than 30 (like 20) normally indicates too light an oil, or worn bearing clearances in mains and rods, or possiblly weak oil pump/ clogged screen.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 09-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Tonker||09-06-2001||12:01 AM||||Thanks guys. Looks like it's working now. Sits around 275 kpa when idling and warm near 500 when driving. ||||reg||1
